    "available_at": "2026-11-06T17:10:15.108593+00:00",
    "body": "China has blocked SoftBank from buying ABB’s robotics business in the country, putting the $5.4 billion deal for ABB’s global robotics division in doubt. ABB, a Swiss industrial equipment maker, confirmed the decision on Nov. 5. It’s unclear whether the companies can carve out the Chinese business or otherwise restructure the deal.\nWhat’s Related\n“ABB and SoftBank are in close dialogue and are working constructively on the transaction,” ABB said. The Swiss company said SoftBank, which is responsible for securing regulatory approvals, had informed it of the decision by China’s foreign investment security review office. The office sits within China’s National Development and Reform Commission.\nWhy China said no\nThe Financial Times, which first reported the decision , said China’s competition review passed in February before the deal underwent a separate foreign investment security review. The newspaper reported that officials examined ABB’s Chinese supply chains and wanted some of the division’s robot manufacturing to stay in China.\nReuters reported that Chinese authorities were concerned about sensitive data being transferred to the United States. SoftBank declined to comment to Reuters, and the commission did not immediately respond to its request for comment.\nWhat’s next\nSoftBank signed the agreement in October 2025 and originally expected it to close in mid-to-late 2026, subject to regulatory approvals. The deal covers a business that designs, makes, and sells industrial robots and had about 7,000 employees when SoftBank announced the purchase.\nChina is ABB’s largest robotics market and accounts for about 30% of the division’s business. ABB opened a $150 million robotics factory and research center in Shanghai in 2022. The Financial Times reported that ABB makes more than 90% of the robots it sells to Chinese customers in China.\nABB and SoftBank are still working on the sale. Before signing with SoftBank, ABB had planned to list its robotics division as a standalone company in the second quarter of 2026. ABB dropped that plan when it agreed to the sale and gave no indication in its Nov. 5 update that it would revive it.",
